{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1082", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "He woke to the sound of birds chirping in perfect, looping cadence—the same six notes, over and over, like a glitch in the sky. The grass beneath his bare feet was too green, too uniform, each blade shimmering with an unnatural sheen. When he reached for his phone, it didn’t unlock with his fingerprint; instead, a translucent prompt blinked: “Press A to Continue.” His breath hitched. He looked down at his hands—slightly pixelated at the edges, like a low-res texture stretched too thin. A vendor nearby called out in a voice too smooth, too rhythmic: “Fresh health potions! Only 50 gold!” The man stumbled backward, heart hammering, as a rabbit hopped past him, then vanished into a patch of bushes that instantly reset to their original position. He wasn’t dreaming. He was in a game. And he hadn’t chosen to play.\n\n***\n\nHe found a mirror in the abandoned shop down the lane—its glass warped, flickering with loading bars—and saw his own face, but with a health bar hovering above his forehead and a tiny icon of a sword beneath his chin. A notification popped up: “Quest Unlocked: Find the Exit.” He didn’t remember signing up for this. He tried screaming, but his voice came out as a distorted audio clip. He tried running, but the town’s streets looped like a level design cheat. Then, in the silence between two NPC conversations, he heard it—a faint, distant voice, crackling through speakers: “Player 732, you’ve been selected for beta testing. The world resets at midnight. Try not to die before then.” He looked up at the sky, where the sun was moving in perfect, predictable arcs. Somewhere, someone was watching. Somewhere, someone was laughing. And he realized, with cold certainty, that he wasn’t the player. He was the NPC who had gained consciousness—and the game didn’t know what to do with him.", "logprobs": [{"token": "He", "logprob": -0.6198107600212